1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:03,773
Player one,

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:05,443
you're about to enter a game you

3
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:06,956
didn't know you were playing.

4
00:00:07,216 --> 00:00:08,422
It's called credit.

5
00:00:08,422 --> 00:00:10,898
And your score decides whether lenders,

6
00:00:10,898 --> 00:00:12,930
landlords, and insurers say yes,

7
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:15,152
and what it costs you when they do.

8
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:17,588
Most people never learn the rules.

9
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:19,444
Today, you'll learn all five.

10
00:00:20,844 --> 00:00:22,783
Welcome back to Broke to Balance.

11
00:00:23,043 --> 00:00:23,812
Quick note:

12
00:00:23,812 --> 00:00:25,211
this is educational,

13
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:27,309
not personal financial advice.

14
00:00:27,309 --> 00:00:30,037
I'll use the common FICO scale from the

15
00:00:30,037 --> 00:00:32,065
United States as the example,

16
00:00:32,065 --> 00:00:35,283
and other scoring models and countries differ.

17
00:00:36,683 --> 00:00:38,421
First, what is a credit score?

18
00:00:38,681 --> 00:00:41,281
It's a three-digit number that summarizes

19
00:00:41,281 --> 00:00:43,247
how risky you look to a lender.

20
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:44,833
On the most common scale,

21
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:48,004
it runs from three hundred to eight hundred fifty.

22
00:00:48,264 --> 00:00:51,337
Lenders use it to decide whether to approve you,

23
00:00:51,337 --> 00:00:53,450
and what interest rate to charge.

24
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:55,435
Landlords and some insurers may

25
00:00:55,435 --> 00:00:56,972
look at your credit too.

26
00:00:57,232 --> 00:01:00,106
A higher score can mean lower interest rates,

27
00:01:00,106 --> 00:01:01,320
and over the years,

28
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:03,811
that can save you thousands of dollars.

29
00:01:05,211 --> 00:01:06,975
Here are the FICO ranges.

30
00:01:07,235 --> 00:01:10,268
Three hundred to five seventy-nine is poor.

31
00:01:10,268 --> 00:01:12,947
Five eighty to six sixty-nine is fair.

32
00:01:12,947 --> 00:01:15,839
Six seventy to seven thirty-nine is good.

33
00:01:16,099 --> 00:01:19,105
Seven forty to seven ninety-nine is very good.

34
00:01:19,105 --> 00:01:21,719
And eight hundred and up is exceptional.

35
00:01:21,979 --> 00:01:23,729
If you're lower than you'd like,

36
00:01:23,729 --> 00:01:24,385
don't panic.

37
00:01:24,385 --> 00:01:25,042
Scores move,

38
00:01:25,042 --> 00:01:27,065
and you're about to learn the levers.

39
00:01:28,465 --> 00:01:29,763
Why does this matter?

40
00:01:29,763 --> 00:01:31,865
Let's compare two borrowers taking

41
00:01:31,865 --> 00:01:34,028
the same twenty thousand dollar car

42
00:01:34,028 --> 00:01:35,326
loan over five years.

43
00:01:35,586 --> 00:01:37,971
Sam has a score around seven sixty,

44
00:01:37,971 --> 00:01:39,879
and gets a six percent rate.

45
00:01:39,879 --> 00:01:42,196
Alex has a score around six forty,

46
00:01:42,196 --> 00:01:43,832
and gets twelve percent.

47
00:01:43,832 --> 00:01:45,808
Those rates are illustrative,

48
00:01:45,808 --> 00:01:47,443
but the pattern is real.

49
00:01:47,703 --> 00:01:49,575
Sam pays about three hundred

50
00:01:49,575 --> 00:01:51,513
eighty-seven dollars a month,

51
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:54,654
and about thirty-two hundred in total interest.

52
00:01:54,654 --> 00:01:57,862
Alex pays about four hundred forty-five a month,

53
00:01:57,862 --> 00:02:00,669
and about sixty-seven hundred in interest.

54
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:01,601
Same car.

55
00:02:01,601 --> 00:02:02,348
Same loan.

56
00:02:02,348 --> 00:02:04,961
A score gap that costs Alex roughly

57
00:02:04,961 --> 00:02:07,425
thirty-five hundred dollars more.

58
00:02:08,825 --> 00:02:11,263
Here's the recipe behind a FICO score.

59
00:02:11,523 --> 00:02:14,303
Payment history is thirty-five percent.

60
00:02:14,303 --> 00:02:15,943
Amounts owed is thirty.

61
00:02:15,943 --> 00:02:18,509
Length of credit history is fifteen.

62
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:19,793
New credit is ten.

63
00:02:19,793 --> 00:02:21,361
And credit mix is ten.

64
00:02:21,621 --> 00:02:23,659
Other models, like VantageScore,

65
00:02:23,659 --> 00:02:25,825
weigh things a little differently,

66
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:27,799
but the big ideas are the same.

67
00:02:28,059 --> 00:02:29,030
Five factors.

68
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:29,925
Five levels.

69
00:02:29,925 --> 00:02:30,747
Let's play.

70
00:02:32,147 --> 00:02:33,913
Level one, the biggest boss:

71
00:02:33,913 --> 00:02:34,922
payment history,

72
00:02:34,922 --> 00:02:37,067
thirty-five percent of your score.

73
00:02:37,327 --> 00:02:38,799
The question is simple:

74
00:02:38,799 --> 00:02:40,015
do you pay on time?

75
00:02:40,015 --> 00:02:43,215
Even one payment that's thirty days late can hurt,

76
00:02:43,215 --> 00:02:45,391
and late payments can stay on your

77
00:02:45,391 --> 00:02:47,247
report for up to seven years.

78
00:02:47,507 --> 00:02:48,531
The fix is easy.

79
00:02:48,531 --> 00:02:51,090
Set up autopay for at least the minimum,

80
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:53,969
and add a calendar reminder for the due date.

81
00:03:00,969 --> 00:03:01,713
Level two:

82
00:03:01,713 --> 00:03:03,872
amounts owed, thirty percent.

83
00:03:03,872 --> 00:03:06,851
The big idea here is credit utilization.

84
00:03:07,111 --> 00:03:09,063
That's how much of your available

85
00:03:09,063 --> 00:03:10,246
credit you're using.

86
00:03:10,246 --> 00:03:12,553
If your limit is three thousand dollars

87
00:03:12,553 --> 00:03:14,150
and you carry nine hundred,

88
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:15,452
that's thirty percent.

89
00:03:15,712 --> 00:03:18,886
A common guideline is to stay under thirty percent,

90
00:03:18,886 --> 00:03:20,752
and lower is generally better.

91
00:03:21,012 --> 00:03:21,583
Two tips.

92
00:03:21,583 --> 00:03:24,502
Pay down balances before the statement closes.

93
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:26,659
And if you ask for a higher limit,

94
00:03:26,659 --> 00:03:28,625
don't spend more because of it.

95
00:03:30,025 --> 00:03:30,774
Level three:

96
00:03:30,774 --> 00:03:33,392
length of credit history, fifteen percent.

97
00:03:33,652 --> 00:03:35,846
Lenders like a long track record.

98
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:38,572
The average age of your accounts matters,

99
00:03:38,572 --> 00:03:41,962
and so does how long your oldest one has been open.

100
00:03:42,222 --> 00:03:44,088
So keep old accounts open if

101
00:03:44,088 --> 00:03:45,688
they have no annual fee,

102
00:03:45,688 --> 00:03:48,154
and use them for a small purchase now

103
00:03:48,154 --> 00:03:50,087
and then to keep them active.

104
00:03:51,487 --> 00:03:52,225
Level four:

105
00:03:52,225 --> 00:03:53,833
new credit, ten percent.

106
00:03:54,093 --> 00:03:55,728
When you apply for credit,

107
00:03:55,728 --> 00:03:57,676
the lender does a hard inquiry,

108
00:03:57,676 --> 00:03:59,813
which can dip your score a little.

109
00:03:59,813 --> 00:04:02,831
Several applications in a short time can add up.

110
00:04:03,091 --> 00:04:05,760
Checking your own score is a soft inquiry,

111
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,031
and it doesn't hurt.

112
00:04:07,031 --> 00:04:08,938
So check as often as you like.

113
00:04:10,338 --> 00:04:11,097
Level five:

114
00:04:11,097 --> 00:04:12,753
credit mix, ten percent.

115
00:04:13,013 --> 00:04:15,202
Lenders like to see you can handle

116
00:04:15,202 --> 00:04:16,876
different kinds of credit:

117
00:04:16,876 --> 00:04:19,193
revolving credit, like credit cards,

118
00:04:19,193 --> 00:04:20,610
and installment loans,

119
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:22,670
like a car loan or student loan.

120
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:24,864
But please don't take out a loan

121
00:04:24,864 --> 00:04:26,376
just to improve your mix.

122
00:04:26,376 --> 00:04:27,706
It's only ten percent,

123
00:04:27,706 --> 00:04:30,064
and debt you don't need isn't worth it.

124
00:04:37,464 --> 00:04:39,307
So how do you raise your score?

125
00:04:39,307 --> 00:04:41,685
Think of it as a game plan, not a trick.

126
00:04:41,945 --> 00:04:43,208
Start with autopay,

127
00:04:43,208 --> 00:04:45,070
so every payment is on time.

128
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:47,431
Next, bring your balances down,

129
00:04:47,431 --> 00:04:49,193
so your utilization drops.

130
00:04:49,453 --> 00:04:51,966
Avoid opening several accounts at once,

131
00:04:51,966 --> 00:04:54,028
and dispute any errors you find.

132
00:04:54,288 --> 00:04:55,388
Then be patient.

133
00:04:55,388 --> 00:04:57,795
Most changes take months, not days,

134
00:04:57,795 --> 00:04:59,514
and good habits compound.

135
00:05:00,914 --> 00:05:02,075
One more power-up:

136
00:05:02,075 --> 00:05:04,074
protect your credit from fraud.

137
00:05:04,334 --> 00:05:05,589
In the United States,

138
00:05:05,589 --> 00:05:07,442
you can freeze your credit with

139
00:05:07,442 --> 00:05:08,697
each bureau for free,

140
00:05:08,697 --> 00:05:10,431
which stops new accounts from

141
00:05:10,431 --> 00:05:11,985
being opened in your name.

142
00:05:12,245 --> 00:05:14,275
You can also set up fraud alerts,

143
00:05:14,275 --> 00:05:17,290
and review your statements and reports regularly.

144
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:19,642
If you spot something you didn't do,

145
00:05:19,642 --> 00:05:20,862
report it right away.

146
00:05:22,262 --> 00:05:24,218
Now, how do you see where you stand?

147
00:05:24,478 --> 00:05:25,879
In the United States,

148
00:05:25,879 --> 00:05:28,950
you can get free credit reports from the three

149
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:31,620
major bureaus at AnnualCreditReport.com.

150
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:33,251
Read them for mistakes,

151
00:05:33,251 --> 00:05:35,159
like accounts that aren't yours,

152
00:05:35,159 --> 00:05:36,768
and dispute anything wrong.

153
00:05:37,028 --> 00:05:39,385
Many banks and card issuers also show

154
00:05:39,385 --> 00:05:41,042
a free score in their app.

155
00:05:42,442 --> 00:05:44,108
What if you're starting from scratch?

156
00:05:44,368 --> 00:05:46,341
Three common ways to start:

157
00:05:46,341 --> 00:05:47,438
a secured card,

158
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:49,558
where you put down a deposit.

159
00:05:49,558 --> 00:05:51,166
A credit-builder loan.

160
00:05:51,166 --> 00:05:53,725
Or becoming an authorized user on a

161
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:55,333
trusted person's card.

162
00:05:55,593 --> 00:05:56,836
Whatever you choose,

163
00:05:56,836 --> 00:05:59,010
put one small recurring bill on it,

164
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:01,433
and pay the full statement every month.

165
00:06:02,833 --> 00:06:04,548
Before we finish, four myths.

166
00:06:04,808 --> 00:06:05,397
Myth one:

167
00:06:05,397 --> 00:06:07,752
carrying a balance helps your score.

168
00:06:07,752 --> 00:06:08,472
It doesn't.

169
00:06:08,472 --> 00:06:10,238
It only costs you interest.

170
00:06:10,238 --> 00:06:11,873
Pay in full when you can.

171
00:06:12,133 --> 00:06:12,667
Myth two:

172
00:06:12,667 --> 00:06:14,622
checking your own score hurts it.

173
00:06:14,622 --> 00:06:16,814
That's a soft inquiry, so it doesn't.

174
00:06:17,074 --> 00:06:17,837
Myth three:

175
00:06:17,837 --> 00:06:19,988
closing old cards always helps.

176
00:06:19,988 --> 00:06:22,139
It can shorten your history and

177
00:06:22,139 --> 00:06:23,735
raise your utilization.

178
00:06:23,995 --> 00:06:24,928
And myth four:

179
00:06:24,928 --> 00:06:27,194
your income is part of your score.

180
00:06:27,194 --> 00:06:27,794
It isn't.

181
00:06:27,794 --> 00:06:30,593
Your credit report doesn't include income.

182
00:06:31,993 --> 00:06:32,999
Let's recap.

183
00:06:33,259 --> 00:06:35,132
Your score has five factors:

184
00:06:35,132 --> 00:06:37,139
payment history, amounts owed,

185
00:06:37,139 --> 00:06:39,146
length of history, new credit,

186
00:06:39,146 --> 00:06:40,149
and credit mix.

187
00:06:40,409 --> 00:06:42,536
Pay on time, keep balances low,

188
00:06:42,536 --> 00:06:43,566
and be patient.

189
00:06:43,566 --> 00:06:45,007
Time is on your side.

190
00:06:45,267 --> 00:06:46,721
If this helped, subscribe,

191
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:48,288
and tell me in the comments:

192
00:06:48,288 --> 00:06:50,078
which level is toughest for you?

193
00:06:50,338 --> 00:06:53,086
And if you learned something from this video,

194
00:06:53,086 --> 00:06:55,223
don't forget to watch our next one:

195
00:06:55,223 --> 00:06:58,093
how to budget when you're paid every two weeks,

196
00:06:58,093 --> 00:06:59,498
or your income changes.

197
00:06:59,758 --> 00:07:02,304
This video is for education only and

198
00:07:02,304 --> 00:07:03,931
isn't financial advice.

199
00:07:03,931 --> 00:07:05,204
See you next time.

